## Proof

**Setup.** For each integer $j \geq 2$, the Riemann zeta function satisfies
$$\zeta(j) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n^{-j} = 1 + \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} n^{-j} > 1,$$
so each factor $\zeta(j)^{-1}$ lies strictly in $(0,1)$. Write
$$\zeta(j)^{-1} = 1 - a_j, \qquad a_j := \frac{\zeta(j)-1}{\zeta(j)} \in (0,1).$$
The product is $p = \prod_{j=2}^{\infty}(1-a_j)$.

**Key criterion.** A standard result on infinite products states: if $0 < a_j < 1$ for all $j$, then $\prod_{j}(1-a_j) > 0$ if and only if $\sum_{j} a_j < \infty$. (The partial products are positive and decreasing, so the limit is $\geq 0$; it is strictly positive exactly when the sum of the "defects" $a_j$ is summable.)

Thus it suffices to show $\sum_{j=2}^{\infty} a_j < \infty$.

**Bounding $a_j$.** Since $\zeta(j) \geq 1$, we have $a_j = \frac{\zeta(j)-1}{\zeta(j)} \leq \zeta(j)-1$. So it suffices to bound $\zeta(j)-1$.

Using the integral-test comparison (the function $x \mapsto x^{-j}$ is decreasing for $x>0$):
$$\zeta(j) - 1 = \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} n^{-j} \leq 2^{-j} + \sum_{n=3}^{\infty} n^{-j} \leq 2^{-j} + \int_{2}^{\infty} x^{-j}\,dx = 2^{-j} + \frac{2^{1-j}}{j-1}.$$
For $j \geq 2$ we have $\frac{2}{j-1} \leq 2$, hence
$$\zeta(j) - 1 \leq 2^{-j}\!\left(1 + \frac{2}{j-1}\right) \leq 3 \cdot 2^{-j}.$$

**Summability.** Therefore
$$\sum_{j=2}^{\infty} a_j \leq \sum_{j=2}^{\infty} (\zeta(j)-1) \leq 3\sum_{j=2}^{\infty} 2^{-j} = 3 < \infty.$$

**Conclusion.** Since $0 < a_j < 1$ for every $j \geq 2$ and $\sum_{j=2}^{\infty} a_j < \infty$, the infinite product
$$p = \prod_{j=2}^{\infty} \zeta(j)^{-1} = \prod_{j=2}^{\infty}(1-a_j)$$
converges to a **strictly positive** limit. Hence $p > 0$. $\blacksquare$

